WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.887
In this lesson you'll learn just enough

2
00:00:03.887 --> 00:00:05.803
about how a large language model

3
00:00:05.803 --> 00:00:08.729
works to see its surprises coming.

4
00:00:08.729 --> 00:00:09.924
The short version:

5
00:00:09.924 --> 00:00:13.013
it cuts your text into pieces called tokens,

6
00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:16.078
then guesses the next piece, one at a time,

7
00:00:16.078 --> 00:00:18.934
using only what fits on its desk.

8
00:00:18.934 --> 00:00:22.278
It was trained to sound right,

9
00:00:22.278 --> 00:00:24.205
which is not the same as being right.

10
00:00:24.205 --> 00:00:26.167
So lift the hood on the engine

11
00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:27.955
from the last lesson.

12
00:00:27.955 --> 00:00:29.453
You won't find gears.

13
00:00:29.453 --> 00:00:32.740
You'll find a desk covered in tiles.

14
00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:36.336
On the course map, this lesson sits at the Start,

15
00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:39.181
in Orientation: what this course is,

16
00:00:39.181 --> 00:00:41.201
and the lantern you carry.

17
00:00:41.201 --> 00:00:45.206
The lanterns strung over every district are AI.

18
00:00:45.206 --> 00:00:47.714
Most of them run on an engine like this,

19
00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:50.258
so it's worth one look inside.

20
00:00:50.708 --> 00:00:52.635
Before the model reads anything,

21
00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:54.980
your text is cut into tokens.

22
00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:56.513
Picture tiles.

23
00:00:56.513 --> 00:00:59.474
A common word is often one tile.

24
00:00:59.474 --> 00:01:03.967
A long or rare word gets split into a few.

25
00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:06.022
In English, a token averages

26
00:01:06.022 --> 00:01:08.634
about three quarters of a word.

27
00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:10.712
Each tile is really a number;

28
00:01:10.712 --> 00:01:12.930
the model works in numbers.

29
00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.322
So it never sees letters.

30
00:01:15.322 --> 00:01:17.899
Ask it to count the R in strawberry,

31
00:01:17.899 --> 00:01:20.929
and it's looking at two or three tiles,

32
00:01:20.929 --> 00:01:22.694
not ten letters.

33
00:01:22.694 --> 00:01:26.282
Tokens are also how everything is measured:

34
00:01:26.282 --> 00:01:27.803
how much it can read,

35
00:01:27.803 --> 00:01:31.833
how much it can write, and what it costs.

36
00:01:32.282 --> 00:01:34.836
The model reads every tile on the desk

37
00:01:34.836 --> 00:01:37.565
and scores every tile it could lay next.

38
00:01:37.565 --> 00:01:39.387
It lays one.

39
00:01:39.387 --> 00:01:41.524
That tile joins the desk,

40
00:01:41.524 --> 00:01:43.776
and it does the whole thing again,

41
00:01:43.776 --> 00:01:45.680
one tile per step,

42
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.430
until it lays a tile that means stop.

43
00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:53.157
That's why answers appear word by word.

44
00:01:53.157 --> 00:01:56.442
There's no finished answer hiding backstage;

45
00:01:56.442 --> 00:01:58.648
you're watching tiles land.

46
00:01:58.648 --> 00:02:02.387
How it picks is set by a dial called temperature.

47
00:02:02.387 --> 00:02:05.243
Low, and it takes the likeliest tile

48
00:02:05.243 --> 00:02:07.785
nearly every time: predictable.

49
00:02:07.785 --> 00:02:10.874
High, and it reaches further down the list:

50
00:02:10.874 --> 00:02:13.579
varied, sometimes odd.

51
00:02:13.579 --> 00:02:15.982
That's one reason the same question

52
00:02:15.982 --> 00:02:18.211
can get two different answers.

53
00:02:18.211 --> 00:02:21.719
Most apps set the dial for you.

54
00:02:22.169 --> 00:02:25.257
The desk is the context window:

55
00:02:25.257 --> 00:02:27.742
everything the model can see at once.

56
00:02:27.742 --> 00:02:30.238
The engine starts cold every message,

57
00:02:30.238 --> 00:02:32.444
so every turn the app slides the whole

58
00:02:32.444 --> 00:02:34.928
conversation back onto the desk:

59
00:02:34.928 --> 00:02:36.821
the hidden instructions,

60
00:02:36.821 --> 00:02:40.466
your messages, its replies, any files.

61
00:02:40.466 --> 00:02:43.415
Desks are large, but they do fill.

62
00:02:43.415 --> 00:02:45.227
Then the app has to choose:

63
00:02:45.227 --> 00:02:47.641
drop the oldest tiles off the edge,

64
00:02:47.641 --> 00:02:49.871
squash them into a short summary,

65
00:02:49.871 --> 00:02:51.809
or stop with an error.

66
00:02:51.809 --> 00:02:54.665
And because everything is re-sent,

67
00:02:54.665 --> 00:02:59.578
a long chat uses more tokens with every message.

68
00:03:00.028 --> 00:03:02.060
Here's where the picture breaks.

69
00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.869
A desk suggests someone with a library underneath.

70
00:03:04.869 --> 00:03:06.309
There isn't one.

71
00:03:06.309 --> 00:03:09.072
Unless the app hands it a search tool,

72
00:03:09.072 --> 00:03:11.255
the model looks nothing up.

73
00:03:11.255 --> 00:03:13.113
Training taught it which tiles

74
00:03:13.113 --> 00:03:15.376
tend to follow which.

75
00:03:15.376 --> 00:03:18.662
That makes it very good at sounding right,

76
00:03:18.662 --> 00:03:21.228
with no built-in check that it is right.

77
00:03:21.228 --> 00:03:23.631
So when the fact isn't on the desk,

78
00:03:23.631 --> 00:03:25.175
it doesn't go quiet.

79
00:03:25.175 --> 00:03:28.124
It lays the likeliest-looking tiles,

80
00:03:28.124 --> 00:03:30.550
in the same calm voice.

81
00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:32.071
Often that's right.

82
00:03:32.071 --> 00:03:35.345
When it isn't, that's a hallucination:

83
00:03:35.345 --> 00:03:37.841
a confident wrong guess.

84
00:03:37.841 --> 00:03:40.732
It shows up in specifics: citations,

85
00:03:40.732 --> 00:03:43.554
version numbers, file paths, package names.

86
00:03:43.554 --> 00:03:46.363
And anything after its training cutoff,

87
00:03:46.363 --> 00:03:50.068
the snapshot from two lessons ago, is a guess too.

88
00:03:50.519 --> 00:03:52.783
Four surprises you'll meet.

89
00:03:52.783 --> 00:03:55.558
Ask how many times R appears in strawberry,

90
00:03:55.558 --> 00:03:58.124
and some models have answered two.

91
00:03:58.124 --> 00:04:00.399
Tiles, not letters.

92
00:04:00.399 --> 00:04:03.394
A long chat forgets your first instructions,

93
00:04:03.394 --> 00:04:06.601
or an error says the context is too long.

94
00:04:07.063 --> 00:04:08.538
The desk filled up.

95
00:04:08.538 --> 00:04:10.999
Code that installs a package,

96
00:04:10.999 --> 00:04:13.774
or calls a function, that doesn't exist.

97
00:04:13.774 --> 00:04:16.549
A confident guess with a plausible name.

98
00:04:16.549 --> 00:04:19.556
And a bill or usage page counted in tokens,

99
00:04:19.556 --> 00:04:21.297
input and output.

100
00:04:21.297 --> 00:04:24.631
That's the re-sent desk, counted.

101
00:04:25.081 --> 00:04:27.345
So put the facts on the desk.

102
00:04:27.345 --> 00:04:28.912
Paste the exact error,

103
00:04:28.912 --> 00:04:31.850
attach the file, share the docs.

104
00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.521
Same model, same question:

105
00:04:33.521 --> 00:04:37.190
with the real file on the desk, the answer fits.

106
00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.013
Ask where a claim comes from,

107
00:04:39.013 --> 00:04:40.348
then open the source.

108
00:04:40.348 --> 00:04:43.158
And check every specific before you build on it:

109
00:04:43.158 --> 00:04:46.130
the package, the path, the version.

110
00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.812
Safe to ignore for now: transformers,

111
00:04:48.812 --> 00:04:51.261
attention, and the math of training.

112
00:04:51.261 --> 00:04:54.535
AI is the lantern: it lights every district,

113
00:04:54.535 --> 00:04:57.010
but it doesn't walk the streets for you.

114
00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:59.376
Three things to keep.

115
00:04:59.376 --> 00:05:03.114
It guesses the next token, over and over.

116
00:05:03.114 --> 00:05:05.738
Text is cut into pieces, not letters,

117
00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:08.942
and limits and prices count them.

118
00:05:08.942 --> 00:05:11.322
It only sees its desk.

119
00:05:11.322 --> 00:05:14.666
The whole conversation is re-sent every turn,

120
00:05:14.666 --> 00:05:19.067
and the oldest parts fall off when the desk fills.

121
00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:21.331
Sounding right isn't being right.

122
00:05:21.331 --> 00:05:25.069
Put the facts on the desk, ask for sources,

123
00:05:25.069 --> 00:05:27.346
and check every specific.
